SET CODED COMPUTATION IN ISABELLE ZF

Tang Ziyi

1 Scope

This repository formalises deterministic binary Turing machines in
Isabelle/ZF. The independent session Set_Coded_Computation_ZF contains machine
semantics, observational tape equivalence, natural number coding, numeric
evaluation, self input halting undecidability, and bounded workspace
preservation.

The machines have one tape, finite control, and five actions. Finite lists
represent a tape with blank cells beyond the represented ends. The definitions
and theorems are in Turing_Machines_ZF/Core. This session depends only on
Isabelle/ZF.

2 Results

Turing_Rejection.self_halting_not_tm_decidable proves that no machine in the
model decides whether a decoded machine halts on the unary representation of
its own code. Its rejection transformation is a concrete finite instruction
table.

Turing_Evaluator.code_step_correct and Turing_Evaluator.code_steps_correct
relate numeric evaluation to the machine semantics. The evaluator is a
mathematical function on codes. A universal machine implementing it has not
been constructed.

Turing_Tape.steps_config_eq preserves equality of represented contents under
execution. Turing_Workspace.bounded_workspace_history proves that a supplied
number of blank buffer cells protects saved data through every execution
prefix within that number of steps. The theorem does not compute a bound on
halting time.

The theory map, assumptions, and research boundaries are recorded in
documentation/DEVELOPMENT.txt.

3 Build

Install Isabelle2025-2. From the repository root, run the following command.

    isabelle build -v -D Turing_Machines_ZF/Core Set_Coded_Computation_ZF

The full repository contains four sessions. The extended machine session
includes concrete programs and primitive recursive certificates. Turing_CH and
Turing_Models contain separate model theoretic research and require AFP
2026-02-06. Complete reproduction instructions and dependency checksums are in
documentation/BUILD.txt.

4 Research boundary

General machine compilation for primitive recursive composition and recursion
remains open. The internal halting formula is still an assumed interface in
Turing_CH. The arithmetic formulas do not yet form a complete effective
translation of arithmetic syntax. These are outside the independent
computation session.

The motivating EPQ paper is papers/EPQ.pdf. Its final reduction to model
invariant sentences has not been fully formalised. Historical reviews and
design notes are indexed in documentation/history/README. Current instructions
and review records are in documentation/records.

5 Attribution and rights

The five action machine design follows the operational model of the AFP entry
Universal Turing Machine, formalised in Isabelle/HOL. This development proves
its results in Isabelle/ZF and does not inherit that entry's universality
theorem. The model theoretic research uses the AFP entry Independence of the
Continuum Hypothesis.

Tang Ziyi formulated the research question and wrote the original EPQ argument
and initial formalisation. Subsequent definitions, theorem statements, proofs,
and repository engineering include substantial AI assistance, recorded in
AI_USAGE.txt. Automated checks do not constitute independent expert review or
establish novelty.

